  "transcript": "# HM Treasury and the economics of climate and nature\n\n## Overall view: policies are mixed and timing matters\n> All three industries welcome some support but say tax and fiscal policy is inconsistent, sends mixed signals, and often raises costs that harm competitiveness.\n\nChair: We are looking at whether Treasury tax and fiscal policies give industries clear incentives to invest in lower-carbon practices. Gentlemen, your quick verdict?\nMatthew Croucher: Short answer: no. There are helpful measures—DRIVE35 support for EVs, for example—but overall the UK is an expensive place to do business, particularly because energy costs are high.\nAndrew McDermott: From ceramics' view it is similar: the framework isn't coherent. Policies add cost to meet environmental aims but often weaken competitiveness and can push production abroad rather than cut global emissions.\nAdam Berman: Treasury understands the opportunity of the energy transition and has backed supply-side schemes, but the demand side—getting homes and businesses to electrify—isn't consistently supported in fiscal policy.\nChair: So the common theme is inconsistent signals and high energy costs limiting investment in decarbonisation?\nMatthew Croucher: Yes—especially timing. The eVED announcement in 2028 came when the industry and consumers need encouragement, not a new tax signal.\n\n\n## Motors, EVs and taxation: mixed messages and practical barriers\n> Manufacturers back electrification but warn that planning, taxes, charging costs and residual values still make the transition costly and uncertain.\n\nChair: Matthew, the SMMT represents the whole motor industry. Do Treasury policies support your members to move to EVs while keeping the industry competitive?\nMatthew Croucher: Not coherently. Support has been stop-start: zero-rated VED and the plug-in grant were removed and then partly reinstated. eVED due in 2028 looks like an EV tax at the wrong time.\nJonathan Davies: Should the UK have pushed more for hydrogen like Japan, rather than following the global move to battery electrics?\nMatthew Croucher: Industry is split but the global trend is towards electrification. Hydrogen has niche uses; infrastructure is almost non-existent here for hydrogen vehicles.\nJonathan Davies: If the ZEV mandate slowed, what would that mean?\nMatthew Croucher: Manufacturers would welcome a gentler trajectory. There are many new BEV models, but consumers still need incentives and public charging is often costly compared with home charging.\nMatthew Croucher: We also need clarity on eVED implementation—things like eVED Connect and how vehicle connectivity data will be used raise privacy and practical questions.\n\n\n## Energy costs and electrification: demand-side support is missing\n> Energy UK says supply-side policy (like Contracts for Difference) has worked, but high electricity prices and weak demand-side incentives are blocking wider electrification.\n\nAdam Berman: Supply-side support has delivered large-scale renewables, but the big gap is demand: households and businesses need clearer long-term incentives to electrify.\nManuela Perteghella: Are the Warm Homes Plan and Boiler Upgrade Scheme enough, especially for off-grid homes?\nAdam Berman: They help, and recent increases for off-grid homes are welcome, but uptake of heat pumps is still tiny and the 'spark gap'—electricity vs gas prices—must close to make electrification broadly attractive.\nManuela Perteghella: What practical steps would encourage people to install heat pumps and retrofit homes?\nAdam Berman: A long-term glide path: stable subsidies that phase down predictably, more installers and accreditation, financing options, and a clear plan to lower electricity operating costs over time.\nAdam Berman: Small, abrupt cuts to programmes and grants create uncertainty. We need a joined-up plan—planning, installers, finance and electricity-costs working together.\n\n\n## Competitiveness, carbon pricing and revenue recycling\n> Taxes that raise costs without targeted recycling risk pushing production abroad; industries ask for level playing fields, clearer carbon-leakage protection and use of revenues to support decarbonisation.\n\nSammy Wilson: Do environmental taxes in your sectors actually change investment, as the Treasury claims?\nAndrew McDermott: Not positively in ceramics. Energy can be up to 50% of production cost; taxes that raise costs make us uncompetitive and drive output offshore rather than fund genuine decarbonisation.\nAndrew McDermott: We see imports rising while domestic output falls. Territorial emissions fall, but consumption emissions—emissions tied to what we buy—can rise. Policy monitoring misses that.\nMatthew Croucher: The motor sector is improving environmental performance, but high UK electricity costs—many times EU averages—make electrifying industrial processes, like paint shops, very expensive.\nAdam Berman: The electricity sector shows a balanced approach can work: carbon pricing plus targeted subsidies like Contracts for Difference drove down emissions from generation steeply.\nSammy Wilson: Should carbon-tax revenue be recycled to help industries decarbonise?\nAndrew McDermott: Yes. The EU ETS has clearer revenue-recycling to support industry. In the UK we have hit-and-miss schemes; more systematic recycling is needed to preserve competitiveness.\nAdam Berman: I agree that a mix of 'stick' and 'carrot' is needed. A carrot-only approach would be far costlier. But revenue use should be targeted to lower operating costs—especially electricity—for industry to invest.\nManuela Perteghella: Could fossil-fuel revenues—like those from Jackdaw—be ringfenced to fund the transition?\nAdam Berman: They could and that makes sense for transition and regional just transition support, but those revenues are relatively small versus the total investment needed—private capital will fund most of it.\nChair: Final practical asks from you three?\nMatthew Croucher: Give industry long-term certainty on tax and regulation, consult more on timing—especially for eVED—and tackle public-charging costs and residual-value risks for EVs.\nAndrew McDermott: Make electricity cheaper for industry, broaden access to schemes like BICS, and adopt metrics that include imports and consumption emissions so policy doesn't simply offshore emissions.\nAdam Berman: Set a clear long-term electrification plan, close the spark gap to favour electricity for heat, support installer capacity and finance, and use targeted revenue recycling to ease the transition.\nChair: Thank you—your evidence on timing, costs, competitiveness and the need for clearer long-term signals will feed into our report.\n",
